**SAMPLE POST for previewing the blog template. It only appears in local development and is never published.**

CRM automation means your CRM updates itself: new leads are created from forms and emails, deals move stages on their own, and follow-ups go out on time without anyone remembering to send them. For a small team, the first two or three automations usually give back 5 to 10 hours a week.

## What CRM automation actually does

Most small teams use their CRM as a place to type things into after the fact. Automation flips that around so the CRM is updated by the events themselves:

- A web form submission **creates the contact and the deal**.
- A reply to your quote email **moves the deal to "In discussion"**.
- A signed contract **marks the deal won and starts onboarding**.

### Where the time goes today

In the projects we have scoped, the biggest losses are re-typing lead details, chasing follow-ups that slipped, and building the Monday report by hand.

## The five workflows to automate first

| Workflow | Typical time saved | Difficulty |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Form to CRM with instant reply | 2–4 h/week | Easy |
| Lead scoring and routing | 1–3 h/week | Medium |
| Follow-up sequences | 2–3 h/week | Easy |
| Deal stage updates from email | 1–2 h/week | Medium |
| Weekly pipeline report | 1–2 h/week | Easy |

## How to roll it out safely

Start with one workflow, build it in a test copy of your setup, and run real examples through it before switching it on. See [how we work](/how-we-work) for the full process.

> Automate the boring, repeatable steps first. Keep people on the steps that need judgment.

For tool choice, Zapier's own [guide to CRM automation](https://zapier.com/blog/crm-automation/) is a good neutral overview.
